{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"b19685ef-3168-4905-98b0-81bb278aaad7","status":"SUCCESS","response":"Two of your posts in the `devto-articles` index directly compare a CPU service against an NVIDIA L4 GPU service (both deploying **EmbeddingGemma 2** on Google Cloud Run):\n\n1. **[EmbeddingGemma 2 on Cloud Run: an int8 CPU Service Against an NVIDIA L4](https://dev.to/gde/embeddinggemma-2-on-cloud-run-an-int8-cpu-service-against-an-nvidia-l4-15ac)** *(published 2026-10-13)*\n   - **What it compares:** Three Cloud Run services built from one Dockerfile—an int8/int4 repacked CPU service (`emb-cpu-repack`, 1 vCPU / 2 GiB, request-billed), a float32 reference CPU service (`emb-cpu-original`, 1 vCPU / 4 GiB, request-billed), and a bf16 GPU service (`emb-l4-original`, 4 vCPU / 16 GiB / 1× NVIDIA L4, instance-billed).\n   - **Key findings:**\n     - **Throughput \u0026 Latency:** The 1 vCPU `cpu-repack` service answers a single query in a median **121 ms** (vs. **251 ms** on float32 CPU and **56 ms** on the L4) and embeds **1.1 docs/s**, whereas the L4 embeds **333.1 docs/s** (**297×** faster than the CPU repack).\n     - **Cost \u0026 Break-Even:** Because the CPU service is billed per request (scaling to zero with no idle cost) and the L4 is billed per instance-second ($1.0465/hr), `cpu-repack` is cheapest for individual search queries (**$3.91 / 1M queries** vs. **$16.27** on the L4), while a busy L4 is cheapest for corpus indexing (**$0.87 / 1M documents** vs. **$25.87** on `cpu-repack`), breaking even once the L4 is kept busy more than **3.4%** of the time it is up.\n\n2. **[EmbeddingGemma 2 on Cloud Run: Minimum Specs, and When an NVIDIA L4 Pays for Itself](https://dev.to/gde/embeddinggemma-2-on-cloud-run-minimum-specs-and-when-an-nvidia-l4-pays-for-itself-5c1k)** *(published 2026-10-15)*\n   - **What it compares:** A follow-up sizing and burst-cost analysis comparing minimum-spec 1 vCPU Cloud Run services (`cpu-repack` int8 on 1 vCPU / 2 GiB and `cpu-w4a16` int4 on 1 vCPU / 3 GiB) against an NVIDIA L4 service (`emb-l4-original`), factoring in actual billed instance time (`billable_instance_time` from Cloud Monitoring, including scale-to-zero idle tail) and end-to-end client throughput.\n   - **Key findings:**\n     - **Minimum CPU Spec:** **1 vCPU and 2 GiB RAM** with the int8 repack is the minimum viable spec (1 GiB OOMs; long 512-token documents require `batch_size \u003c= 8` on 2 GiB or 4 GiB RAM). The int4 repack (`cpu-w4a16`) is 1.52× slower per query on CPU (**201 ms** vs. **133 ms**) because weights unpack to float32 at load.\n     - **Burst Break-Even vs. L4:** Each isolated cold burst on the L4 bills an average of **474 seconds** (~8 minutes, or **$0.14**) including idle scale-down time, and reaches **171.8 docs/s** end-to-end from a client (**$1.69 / 1M docs** while busy vs. **$27.67 / 1M docs** on the 1 vCPU int8 service). A single burst must exceed **5,307 documents** before the NVIDIA L4 costs less than the 1 vCPU int8 service; keeping an L4 warm all month costs **$764/month**, equivalent to embedding **27.6 million documents** on the CPU service.\n","duration_seconds":138.083717292,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":82022,"output_tokens":4429,"thinking_tokens":2213,"cache_read_tokens":407720,"total_tokens":86451}}}
